{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-36559" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that a function $f(x,y,z)$ and all of its higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ and that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂z}=3{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xz)+3x{y}^{2}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz)+3{x}^{2}{z}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\n$$\n\nEvaluate the following expression at the point $(\\pi ,-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂z∂y∂y}+\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂y∂z∂z}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060742/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4d0d77c2372960bbb2cb92b81f00355ccf51afc0a35839abb2128c8948ff2b1"} {"question" "q-36559" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "As the higher order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂z∂y∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂y∂y∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂z}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xz)+3x{y}^{2}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz)+3{x}^{2}{z}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & =6y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xz)+6xyz\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz)-3{x}^{3}{z}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn addition, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂y∂z∂z} & =\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂z∂y∂z} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂z}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(3{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xz)+3x{y}^{2}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz)+3{x}^{2}{z}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & =3x{y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz)+3x{y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xz)-3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}z\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xz)+6{x}^{2}z\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate both partial derivatives at the point $(\\pi ,-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂z∂y∂y}{|}_{(\\pi ,-1,1)} & =6(-1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )+6(\\pi )(-1)(1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )-3(\\pi )^{3}(1)^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi )=6\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂y∂z∂z}{|}_{(\\pi ,-1,1)} & =3(\\pi )(-1)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )+3(\\pi )(-1)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )-3(\\pi )^{2}(-1)^{2}(1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )+6(\\pi )^{2}(1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\pi )=-6\\pi -6{\\pi }^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂z∂y∂y}{|}_{(\\pi ,-1,1)}+\\frac{{∂}^{3}f}{∂y∂z∂z}{|}_{(\\pi ,-1,1)} & =6\\pi +(-6\\pi -6{\\pi }^{2})=-6{\\pi }^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060742/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4d0d77c2372960bbb2cb92b81f00355ccf51afc0a35839abb2128c8948ff2b1"} {"question" "q-36559" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060742/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "13c1ccafe7d5dc22549f316019a7c7a5ff784e86e316672eec6721d4639f3bbd"} {"question" "q-36559" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060742/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4d0d77c2372960bbb2cb92b81f00355ccf51afc0a35839abb2128c8948ff2b1"} {"question" "q-36559" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-12\\pi +6{\\pi }^{2}"] ["math" "-3{\\pi }^{3}-6{\\pi }^{2}"] ["math" "-6{\\pi }^{2}"] ["math" "12\\pi -6{\\pi }^{2}"] ["math" "3{\\pi }^{3}-6{\\pi }^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060742/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4d0d77c2372960bbb2cb92b81f00355ccf51afc0a35839abb2128c8948ff2b1"} {"question" "q-36559" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-6{\\pi }^{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060742/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4d0d77c2372960bbb2cb92b81f00355ccf51afc0a35839abb2128c8948ff2b1"} {"question" "q-80652" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=4x+{y}^{2}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=4xy-y$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=2x+y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=x+\\sqrt{y}$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=2xy+{y}^{2}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)={x}^{2}y+2x$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060742/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4d0d77c2372960bbb2cb92b81f00355ccf51afc0a35839abb2128c8948ff2b1"} {"question" "q-80652" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x+{y}^{2})=2y \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4xy-y)=4y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+y)=1 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+\\sqrt{y})=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy+{y}^{2})=2x+2y \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y+2x)=2xy+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$,  the third pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II only'." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060742/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4d0d77c2372960bbb2cb92b81f00355ccf51afc0a35839abb2128c8948ff2b1"} {"question" "q-80652" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060742/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "13c1ccafe7d5dc22549f316019a7c7a5ff784e86e316672eec6721d4639f3bbd"} {"question" "q-80652" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060742/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4d0d77c2372960bbb2cb92b81f00355ccf51afc0a35839abb2128c8948ff2b1"} {"question" "q-80652" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060742/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4d0d77c2372960bbb2cb92b81f00355ccf51afc0a35839abb2128c8948ff2b1"} {"question" "q-80652" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060742/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4d0d77c2372960bbb2cb92b81f00355ccf51afc0a35839abb2128c8948ff2b1"} {"question" "q-80759" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)={e}^{-2x}-y$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)={e}^{-y}-2x$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=5$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=2{y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy+{y}^{2}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡xy+2xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy+2xy$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060742/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4d0d77c2372960bbb2cb92b81f00355ccf51afc0a35839abb2128c8948ff2b1"} {"question" "q-80759" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{-2x}-y)=-1 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{-y}-2x)=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=0 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{y}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy+{y}^{2})=4y\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy-2x{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡xy+2y \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡xy+2xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy+2xy) \\\\  & =2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy+2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy-2x{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡xy+2y \\\\  & =4y\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy-2x{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡xy+2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$,  the third pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II and III only'." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060742/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4d0d77c2372960bbb2cb92b81f00355ccf51afc0a35839abb2128c8948ff2b1"} {"question" "q-80759" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060742/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "13c1ccafe7d5dc22549f316019a7c7a5ff784e86e316672eec6721d4639f3bbd"} {"question" "q-80759" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060742/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4d0d77c2372960bbb2cb92b81f00355ccf51afc0a35839abb2128c8948ff2b1"} {"question" "q-80759" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I, II and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060742/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4d0d77c2372960bbb2cb92b81f00355ccf51afc0a35839abb2128c8948ff2b1"} {"question" "q-80759" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060742/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4d0d77c2372960bbb2cb92b81f00355ccf51afc0a35839abb2128c8948ff2b1"} {"question" "q-80762" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U.$ Which of the following pairs could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y)?$\n\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=2x-2{y}^{2}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=2y-2xy$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{x})$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)={e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$\n- ${f}_{x}(x,y)=3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}$ and ${f}_{y}(x,y)=2{x}^{3}y-\\sqrt{y}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060742/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4d0d77c2372960bbb2cb92b81f00355ccf51afc0a35839abb2128c8948ff2b1"} {"question" "q-80762" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x-2{y}^{2})=-4y \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y-2xy)=-2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{x}))=0 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}{y}^{2})=6{x}^{2}y \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{3}y-\\sqrt{y})=6{x}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$,  the third pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II and III only'." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060742/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4d0d77c2372960bbb2cb92b81f00355ccf51afc0a35839abb2128c8948ff2b1"} {"question" "q-80762" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060742/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "13c1ccafe7d5dc22549f316019a7c7a5ff784e86e316672eec6721d4639f3bbd"} {"question" "q-80762" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060742/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4d0d77c2372960bbb2cb92b81f00355ccf51afc0a35839abb2128c8948ff2b1"} {"question" "q-80762" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060742/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4d0d77c2372960bbb2cb92b81f00355ccf51afc0a35839abb2128c8948ff2b1"} {"question" "q-80762" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060742/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4d0d77c2372960bbb2cb92b81f00355ccf51afc0a35839abb2128c8948ff2b1"} {"question" "q-133373" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the function $f(x,y)$ and all of its higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ evaluate ${f}_{yxy}(6,\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ if ${f}_{xy}(x,y)=7+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060742/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4d0d77c2372960bbb2cb92b81f00355ccf51afc0a35839abb2128c8948ff2b1"} {"question" "q-133373" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "As the higher-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. Hence, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{yxy}(x,y) & ={f}_{xyy}(x,y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({f}_{xy}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(7+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(6,\\frac{\\pi }{3}):$\n\n$$\n{f}_{yxy}(6,\\frac{\\pi }{3})=6\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=3\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060742/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4d0d77c2372960bbb2cb92b81f00355ccf51afc0a35839abb2128c8948ff2b1"} {"question" "q-133373" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060742/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "13c1ccafe7d5dc22549f316019a7c7a5ff784e86e316672eec6721d4639f3bbd"} {"question" "q-133373" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060742/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4d0d77c2372960bbb2cb92b81f00355ccf51afc0a35839abb2128c8948ff2b1"} {"question" "q-133373" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "3"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060742/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4d0d77c2372960bbb2cb92b81f00355ccf51afc0a35839abb2128c8948ff2b1"} {"question" "q-133373" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060742/state.json" "file_sha256" "f4d0d77c2372960bbb2cb92b81f00355ccf51afc0a35839abb2128c8948ff2b1"}] "usage" {} "basis" 1946 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
